Let $u = x + \frac{1}{x}$. Then $u^2 = x^2 + 2 + \frac{1}{x^2}$, so $x^2 + \frac{1}{x^2} = u^2 - 2$.
Also, $u^3 = x^3 + 3x + \frac{3}{x} + \frac{1}{x^3} = x^3 + \frac{1}{x^3} + 3\left(x + \frac{1}{x}\right) = x^3 + \frac{1}{x^3} + 3u$, so $x^3 + \frac{1}{x^3} = u^3 - 3u$.
We have
\begin{align*} \left( x + \frac{1}{x} \right)^6 &= u^6 \\ x^6 + \frac{1}{x^6} &= \left( x^3 + \frac{1}{x^3} \right)^2 - 2 = (u^3 - 3u)^2 - 2 = u^6 - 6u^4 + 9u^2 - 2 \end{align*}
Then
\begin{align*} \left( x + \frac{1}{x} \right)^6 - \left( x^6 + \frac{1}{x^6} \right) - 2 &= u^6 - (u^6 - 6u^4 + 9u^2 - 2) - 2 \\ &= 6u^4 - 9u^2 \end{align*}
Also,
\begin{align*} \left( x + \frac{1}{x} \right)^3 + \left( x^3 + \frac{1}{x^3} \right) &= u^3 + u^3 - 3u \\ &= 2u^3 - 3u \end{align*}
Therefore,
\begin{align*} \frac{\left( x + \frac{1}{x} \right)^6 - \left( x^6 + \frac{1}{x^6} \right) - 2}{\left( x + \frac{1}{x} \right)^3 + \left( x^3 + \frac{1}{x^3} \right)} &= \frac{6u^4 - 9u^2}{2u^3 - 3u} \\ &= \frac{3u^2(2u^2 - 3)}{u(2u^2 - 3)} \\ &= 3u = 3\left( x + \frac{1}{x} \right) \end{align*}
Since $x > 0$, $x + \frac{1}{x} \ge 2$ by AM-GM. Therefore, $3\left( x + \frac{1}{x} \right) \ge 3(2) = 6$.
The minimum value is 6, which occurs when $x = 1$.

